Java data types and Sting APIs
-------------

- 8 primitive data types in Java:
	int 		32 bits
	short		16
	long		64
	(*) float	32
	double		64
	char		16
	byte		8
	boolean 1bit, size not precisely defined (possibly 1 byte)
		
	- Note: float and double have limited amount of significant digits they can store (7 - float, 16 - double). So for PI - float will be able to store only with precition of 6 fractional digits, whilst double with 15 fractional digits
	Also note, the larger the integer part, the less fractional precision you can store.
	
	- Use BigDecimal if you need to overcome this limitations
	
	- Note: you can assign integral values to float or double - value will be transferred in Scientific notation (mantissa and exponent).
	It will never overflow even with very huge numbers, but loose precision when going beyond maxim representable in mantissa (integral values > 2^24 for float and > 2^53 for double). 

	- char is only positive numbers; declared with ''; for Unicode using hexadecimal codes like '\u0634' 
	
	- literals in octal, binary and hexadecimal are written as corr-ly: 0..., 0b..., 0x...
	
	- all primitive types have the counterpart Wrapper classes
	
	- java automatically do Autoboxing and Unboxing between primitive and Wrapper types
	
	- widening and casting do not work between Wrapper classes 
	
- Widening, Narrowing, Overflow - we can cast smaller size primitive type to bigger one; we can cast bigger into smaller is value not exceeds the range; in other cases we get overflow result is unpredictable, including e.g. assign negative short value to char



Operators and Math APIs
-------------

- Operators and Math APIs
	- byte, short with arithmetic operators are promoted to int
	- when operands are different type they are promoted to be longest type capable to hold all of them
	- compound assignments (+= -= etc) widen or cast result to the assigned type (not promoting to the largest type)
	
- Variable rules and scope
	- local variables must be explicitly initialized (partial initialization i.e. initialize on condition won't compile)
	- if member has no access modifier - it's package private i.e. accessible only within class' package (or subpackage) but private outside (not accessible in subclass outside of package)
	- protected is as above + accessible in any subclass
	- local variable of same name as static or instance var will override it
	
	- Note: new variable declaration is using var variable = 5; type is inferred; variable must be initialized; for reference types too, but init with null like this var variable = (String) null; can only be used as local variable; type for var can't be changed
	
- Strings, Dates, and Times
	- Note: String pool - java using SAME object for equal String LITERALS declaration; though this is not the case for constructors or other ways of string creation not from literal declaration, they will not be equal references v1 == v2 i.e. two different objects
	
	- java.date.format.DateTimeFormatter is used to format from java Date Time type. Note to use ISO predefined formatters matching the variable type in hand (otherwise getting UnsupportedTemporalTypeException)
		
		Custom date formatter example: LocalDateTime localDateTime = LocalDateTime.of(2022, 10, 31, 9, 45); var pattern = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("EEEE, LLLL dd, yyyy 'at' hh:mm"); localDateTime.format(pattern);
	

Java OO Approach
-------------
	
- Java OO Approach
	- Note: initializer block (in curly brackets within the class) is executed before call to constructor - contrary to this static initializer block stands has static keyword and can you only static fields/methods
	
	- Garbage collection phases: Mark, Delete, Compact (optionally compact remaining referenced objects)
	
	- Course: Understanding the Java Virtal Machine: Memory Management (Kevin Jones)
	
	- Note: Method signature is method name + parameter list (sequeence of types actually)
	
	- Just a reminder: primitive types are passed by value, whilst reference type - by reference
	
	- Just a reminder: the sub class can in relation to super class: access accessible fields/methods, create new fields/methods different on name, override accessible instance methods (also non-final), invoke constructor
	
	- Note: to call method from super class use super.method()
	
	- Polymorphism - ability of abstraction to take different forms (!) at runtime (you may even not need to recompile) - late binding
		- example: several classes differently implement same method of interface, subclass overriding super class method
		- reduces coupling - compared to inheritance, and lets use abstraction types to express a whole category of implementations
	
	
	- Just a reminder: Abstract classes
		- abstract methods of abstract classes are forced to be overridden in their concrete sub-classes
		- also template design pattern is when you define a non-abstract method of abstract class using inside it abstract methods of this class
		- abstract classes may have constructors, but they can't be instantiated
		
	- Interface can have: method signatures, default methods, static methods, nested types, constants (public). No constructors in interfaces
		- interface methods are abstract and public by default, and it can't be made non-public in implementation
		- Note: default methods serve as one way of keeping contract from break with implementation classes, when interface needs to be modified. Implementing classes may overwrite default though, or may not
		- Note: static methods - just a way to handy keep utility method bound to interface (you wouldn't need a concrete class if utility method stick well to interface)
		
	- Functional interface - interface with a single (abstract) method. Use @FunctionalInterface
		- can you lambda to implement functional interface - implementation on the spot
		- Examples: Predicate<T>, Function<T>, Runnable, Callable, Comparable<T>
	
	- Enums - variable holding certain predefined values - fixed set of constants (actually objects of Enum class)
		- enums are 1-st class passengers with fields, methods and constructors
		- Note: compiler adds special methods values(), valueOf()
		- enum may have fields assigned to each value 
		- enum constructor must be private or package private - it is called automatically for defined values
		- Note: constants in enums should be defined first
		
- Inner (Nested) class - class associated with a instance of the outer class
	- Inner class has direct access to members of outer class
	- Inner class may not contain any static members
	- Instantiate Inner class: Instantiate Outer class object > Instantiate Inner class object, using new method of Outer object
		Outer ou = new Outer(); Inner in = ou.new Inner();
	- Inner class can even be public to be reused
	
- Local class - class defined within the block of code: in method, in for loop, in if clause, or block of code
	- when defined inside member method of Outer class, Local class can access members of Outer class + local variables provided they are final or effectively final (means instantiated by fact only once)  + method parameters. Instantiate this class (only!) within the method and use it
	- Local class can not contain any static members except constants
	- Local class can not be interface
	- Can not be instantiated outside of the block where it is declared
	- Local class can not have access modifiers
	
- Anonymous class - like Local class, created using new <Interface | Base class> { <block for class definition> }. Same rules as for Local above apply to Anonymous class, plus you can not declare constructor in Anonymous class
	- Note: Lambdas effectively are anonymous classes for Functional interfaces (interface with a single method)
	- Course: Using Lambda expressions in Java code - by Jose Paumard
	

Exceptions handling
-------------

- Exception handling
	- In catch blocks you have to respect Exception class hierarchy, i.e. catching more specific exceptions must precede catching more general ones	
	- Multi-catch block is the one using syntax catch (Exception1 |Exception2 e)
		- Exceptions in multi-catch block must be disjoint (can be in hierarchy relation)
	- Just a reminder - finally block is guaranteed to always execute, no matter what (unless you call System.exit() somewhere haha)
	- With a finally present catch blocks become optional

- Try with resources: try (FileInputStream in = new FileInputStream("in.txt"); FileOutputStream out = new FileOutputStream("out.txt")) { <code> }
	- you may have as many resources inside try as you need, separated by ; 
	- java guarantees to close all opened resources before whole construct is completed (actually rewrites it into finally under the hood)
	- Note: try with resources has catch{} and finally{} - optional if resource opening and <code> doesn't throw an exception - otherwise use catch block
	- Note: local variable declaration (of resource) within try - is not available in catch
	- Note: closing of resources is done in order reverse to opening
	- Note: resource is any class implementing AutoClosable functional interface - having method void close() 
	
- Just a reminder: Throwable is the parent of exceptions hierarchy, having Exception and Error subclasses.
- Exceptions categories in: Checked, Unchecked (Runtime is the base class)
- Exceptions to memoize:
	- RuntimeException(s)
		- ArithmeticException (divide by 0)
		- ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException
		- ClassCastException (casting to incompatible type)
		- IllegalArgumentException (throw yourself to signal on bad argument type e.g. negative number where must be non-negative)
		- IllegalStateException
		- NPE
		- NumberFormatException (e.g. trying to convert String into Integer)
		- Note: the majority of above Exceptions directly spring from RuntimeException, but NumberFormatException > IllegalArgumentException > RuntimeException
	- Checked Exception(s)
		(java.io)
		- IOException
		- FileNotfoundException ^
		
		(java.nio)
		- FileSystemException
		- NoSuchFileException
		- AccessDeniedException
		
	- Course: Java Fundamentals: NIO and NIO2
	
- Error - we should never catch, or try to recover
- Error(s) to memoize:
	- ExceptionInitializerError (if static class initializer fails - i.e. static variables initialization, static blocks etc)
	- StackOverflowError
	- NoClassDefFoundError (class missing at runtime, or couldn't be ClassLoaded)
	- OutOfMemoryError
	
- Note: we usually don't care for declaring any RuntimeException(s) in method signatures
- Note: Exceptions are covariant in Java, meaning in @Override method you can narrow down exception compared to type thrown in parent (overriden) method. Also you may skip throwing some exception declared by overriden method in @Override method.
- Note: Remember that "throws" clause is not part of method signature

-Exception handling Clean code principles:
	- Never catch Throwable
	- Prefer catching specific exceptions rather than general
	- Don't swallow exceptions (no empty catch blocks)






